[article Restrict default assignment operators, c++29
	[quickbook 1.7]
	[id restrict-default-assignment-operators-cpp29]
]

[role drop_menu
> [link restrict-default-assignment-operators-cpp29 Start][br]
> [@. Back: Home]
]

Current c++ permits various ways of defaulted assignment operators of class.

Such as these permit implausible declarations:

`
A & operator=(const A &) && = default;
`

`
B & operator=(const B &) const = default;
`

Where A, B are c++ classes.

Proposed c++29 will forbit such declarations.

[h1:some-divergences [role cyan Some divergences]]

[table
	[
		[Code]
		[[role dddred Current]]
		[[role dred Proposed]]
	]
	[
		[`A & operator=(A &) = default;`]
		[OK]
		[[role pblue [*OK]]]
	]
	[
		[
			```
			template <typename>
			class A
			{
			public:
				static const A & fn();
				A & operator=(decltype(A::fn())) = default;
			};
			```
		]
		[
			gcc: Invalid[br]
			msvc: Invalid[br]
			edg: Invalid
		]
		[[role pblue [*OK]]]
	]
	[
		[`A & operator=(const A &&) = default;`]
		[OK]
		[[role pblue [*Invalid]]]
	]
	[
		[`A & operator=(const A &) const = default;`]
		[OK]
		[[role pblue [*Invalid]]]
	]
	[
		[`A & operator=(const A &) && = default;`]
		[OK]
		[[role pblue [*Invalid]]]
	]
	[
		[`A && operator=(const A &) && = default;`]
		[Invalid]
		[[role pblue [*Invalid]]]
	]
	[
		[
			```
			class M
			{
			public:
				M & operator=(const M &) volatile;
			};
			class A
			{
			public:
				volatile M m;
				A & operator=(const A &) = default;
			};
			```
		]
		[Deleted]
		[[role pblue [*Deleted]]]
	]
	[
		[
			```
			class A
			{
			public:
				A & operator=(A &);
			};
			class B
			{
			public:
				A a;
				B & operator=(const B &) = default;
			};
			```
		]
		[Deleted]
		[[role pblue [*Deleted]]]
	]
	[
		[
			```
			class A
			{
			public:
				A & operator=(A &);
			};
			class B
			{
			public:
				A a;
				B & operator=(const B &);
			};
			B & B::operator=(const B &) = default;
			```
		]
		[Invalid]
		[[role pblue [*Invalid]]]
	]
]

